   <notesStmt>
    <note type="project" target="#dat1">An early draft of lines for this poem (uva.00268) is written on the reverse of a letter to Whitman dated 14 August 1889. In his notes of a visit to the poet on 26 February 1890, Horace Traubel writes that Whitman reported "having sent 'A Twilight Song' to the Century." (See <hi rend="italic">With Walt Whitman in Camden</hi> [Carbondale and Edwardsville: Southern Illinois University Press, 1982] 6:311). Therefore, the current manuscript must have been created between these two dates.</note>
    <note type="work_relations" target="xxx.00398" cert="high">
      <p>"A Twilight Song: For unknown buried soldiers, North and South" was first published in the May 1890 issue of <hi rend="italic">Century Illustrated Monthly Magazine</hi>. With minor changes and without the subtitle, it was included in <hi rend="italic">Good-Bye My Fancy</hi> (1891) and in the "Good-Bye My Fancy" annex to <hi rend="italic">Leaves of Grass</hi> (1891-1892).</p>
     </note>
